    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>An <strong>automatic under desk elliptical</strong> is a seated elliptical with a motor inside. Plug it in, pick a speed with the remote, and the pedals carry your feet through a smooth oval path whether you push or not. That one feature changes who the machine is for and what it can realistically do for you.</p>
<p>If you have limited leg strength, stiff joints or a doctor who has told you to keep your legs moving, an automatic elliptical gives you motion without effort. If your goal is calorie burn, the motor is a mixed blessing: it makes moving easy, which also means your muscles may do very little unless you pedal along.</p>
<p>This guide explains how automatic machines work, the difference between passive and active modes, who benefits most, how many calories you can honestly expect, and four automatic models worth checking.</p>

<h2>How an Automatic Under Desk Elliptical Works</h2>
<p>Inside the housing, a small electric motor drives the crank that the two pedals are attached to. When you switch it on, the motor turns the crank at a steady speed. Because the pedals are linked to the crank on an elliptical path, your feet rise, glide forward, drop and slide back, much like a full-size elliptical but compressed for seated use.</p>
<p>Most automatic models share the same handful of controls:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Speed settings:</strong> several preset speeds, from a slow shuffle to a brisk pace. The motor holds that speed for you.</li>
<li><strong>Forward and reverse:</strong> reversing the direction shifts some of the work toward the calves and hamstrings. The [[amazon:lifepro-autoglide|Lifepro AutoGlide]] lists powered forward and reverse motion.</li>
<li><strong>Remote control:</strong> so you can change speed without bending under the desk.</li>
<li><strong>Timer or display:</strong> to track session time and estimated strides.</li>
</ul>
<p>The important detail is that the motor sets the pace. On a manual machine, the pace is whatever your legs produce. On an automatic one, your legs follow the machine.</p>

<h2>Passive vs Active Mode: The Difference That Matters</h2>
<p>Every automatic under desk elliptical can be used in two very different ways, and knowing which one you are doing explains most of the confusion in owner reviews.</p>
<h3>Passive mode</h3>
<p>You rest your feet on the pedals and let the motor move them. Your hips, knees and ankles bend and straighten through their range of motion, but your muscles are mostly along for the ride. This is the "automatic elliptical" experience most people picture, and it is closer to a gentle range-of-motion exercise than a workout.</p>
<h3>Active (assisted) mode</h3>
<p>You let the motor run and push the pedals along with it, or slightly ahead of it. Your quads, glutes and calves now do real work, and the motor keeps the rhythm steady so you do not slow down when you get absorbed in an email. Some machines, such as the [[amazon:yosuda-electric|YOSUDA electric elliptical]], also let you switch the motor off and pedal fully manually.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Passive mode</th><th>Active mode</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who moves the pedals</td><td>The motor</td><td>You and the motor together</td></tr>
<tr><td>Muscle work</td><td>Very light</td><td>Light to moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calorie burn</td><td>Close to sitting</td><td>Meaningfully higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Stiffness, mobility, circulation goals</td><td>Daily activity and calorie goals</td></tr>
<tr><td>Focus while working</td><td>Easy, almost no attention needed</td><td>Easy once the rhythm is familiar</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> start every session passive for a few minutes to loosen up, then push along for 10 to 20 minutes, then drop back to passive. You get the warm-up benefit and some real effort without having to think about it.</p></div>

<h2>Who an Automatic Under Desk Elliptical Suits (and Who It Doesn't)</h2>
<h3>Good fit</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Limited mobility or strength.</strong> If pushing pedals against resistance is hard, the motor removes that barrier. Older adults and people returning to activity often find passive motion the easiest way to start.</li>
<li><strong>Circulation and stiffness goals.</strong> Gentle, repeated ankle and knee movement may help your legs feel less heavy and stiff after long periods of sitting. If you have a circulation condition, ask your doctor before you start.</li>
<li><strong>People who forget to move.</strong> Because the motor keeps going, you will not drift to a stop the way you might on a manual machine.</li>
<li><strong>Use from an armchair or recliner.</strong> Many owners use automatic models in front of the TV, not just at a desk.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Not the best fit</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>You want a real calorie burn.</strong> A [[amazon:cubii-jr2|manual Cubii JR2]] or other magnetic machine makes your legs do the work by default.</li>
<li><strong>You need silence for calls.</strong> Even quiet motors hum. Manual magnetic machines are usually quieter. See our <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">quietest under desk elliptical</a> roundup.</li>
<li><strong>No outlet near your desk.</strong> You will need a cord run or an extension, which matters in shared offices.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Health note:</strong> an automatic elliptical is exercise equipment, not a medical device. If you are recovering from surgery, have a joint replacement, neuropathy, a history of blood clots or a heart condition, check with your doctor or physical therapist about speed and session length before you use one.</p></div>

<h2>Automatic Elliptical Calorie Reality</h2>
<p>Marketing for automatic ellipticals often shows calorie counts that only make sense if you are pedaling hard. Here is a more honest way to think about it.</p>
<p>Researchers describe activity in METs (multiples of resting energy). In the Compendium of Physical Activities, quiet sitting is around 1.3 METs, light seated pedaling around 2.0 and moderate pedaling around 2.8. Passive motion, where the motor does the work, sits only a little above quiet sitting because your muscles are barely contracting.</p>
<p>For a 170 lb person, that works out roughly like this:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Sitting still:</strong> about 100 calories an hour.</li>
<li><strong>Passive automatic mode:</strong> only slightly more than sitting.</li>
<li><strong>Light active pedaling:</strong> about 160 calories an hour, or around 55 more than sitting.</li>
<li><strong>Moderate active pedaling:</strong> about 225 calories an hour, or around 120 more than sitting.</li>
</ul>
<p>So the motor itself burns electricity, not body fat. The calories come from you. If weight loss is part of your goal, plan to use active mode for at least part of each session. Plug in your own weight and minutes here:</p>
